Your Lambda code:

=============

def lambda_handler(event, context):

    query = event["query"]

    # RAG
    documents = search_qdrant(query)

    # LLM
    answer = generate_answer(query, documents)

    return {
        "statusCode": 200,
        "body": answer
    }

==============

When the user sends:

"What is the return policy?"

AWS does roughly:

User
  ↓
API Gateway
  ↓
Start Lambda
  ↓
Run Python/RAG
  ↓
Search Qdrant
  ↓
Call LLM
  ↓
Return answer
  ↓
Lambda execution ends

There is no continuously running application server that you manage.

======

The main difference

Think about a restaurant:

Server-based

You keep a restaurant open continuously.

Server: 🟢 RUNNING
Server: 🟢 RUNNING
Server: 🟢 RUNNING
Server: 🟢 RUNNING

Even if nobody is ordering, the server is running.

======

Serverless

The restaurant prepares an order when someone places one.

No request → Lambda not executing

User request
     ↓
Lambda starts
     ↓
Process request
     ↓
Return response
     ↓
Execution finishes

AWS handles the underlying infrastructure.

======

But both can be "real-time"

This is important.

Server-based ≠ real-time
Serverless ≠ not real-time

Both can respond to a user immediately.

====

n your projects

Your existing approach could be:

Voiceflow → FastAPI → RAG → Qdrant → LLM

That's a server-based real-time RAG chatbot if FastAPI is hosted on a continuously running server.

The AWS version from your screenshot is:

Voiceflow → API Gateway → Lambda → RAG → Qdrant → LLM

That's a serverless real-time RAG chatbot.

So the word "real-time" describes how quickly the application responds, while "server-based vs serverless" describes how the backend is deployed.

